WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.219 --> 00:00:02.581
- He's an american singer, song-writer,

00:00:02.581 --> 00:00:05.711
rapper, record producer,
and fashion designer.

00:00:05.711 --> 00:00:07.998
He's worked with a variety
of artists including,

00:00:07.998 --> 00:00:11.043
Shakira, Jennifer Lopez, and Madonna.

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:12.646
He's earned ten Grammy awards,

00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:14.733
including two with the Neptunes.

00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:16.041
He's Pharrell Williams,

00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:18.697
and here are his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:21.693
(fast zooming)

00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:23.246
(R &amp; B music)

00:00:23.246 --> 00:00:25.949
- We wouldn't take on a
project just to take it on

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:28.212
and the couple of times that we did that,

00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:31.566
like, it always just
backfired, like, terribly.

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:34.677
So the lesson there was just
stay loyal to the craft.

00:00:34.677 --> 00:00:38.283
You know, stay loyal to your
creativity because it's a gift.

00:00:38.283 --> 00:00:41.709
Well, I remember being in high school,

00:00:41.709 --> 00:00:43.959
for 20,000 kajillion years,

00:00:48.164 --> 00:00:51.946
And, I just, when we were given
the opportunity, you know,

00:00:51.946 --> 00:00:53.264
a lot of my teachers, of course,

00:00:53.264 --> 00:00:55.361
because they weren't really well-versed

00:00:55.361 --> 00:00:56.843
in, like, the music industry,

00:00:56.843 --> 00:01:00.387
as it was a foreign business to the area.

00:01:00.387 --> 00:01:01.553
They're like, "well, you know, make sure

00:01:01.553 --> 00:01:02.452
you get your education".

00:01:02.452 --> 00:01:04.945
And that, they were right about.

00:01:04.945 --> 00:01:08.945
But they went a little
bit beyond that, it went,

00:01:09.886 --> 00:01:11.893
it went more toward the side of,

00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:13.639
and besides, you know that only lasts

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:16.644
for this long or that long and

00:01:16.644 --> 00:01:18.539
I'm up here talking to you guys right now.

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:21.475
So it just goes to show you

00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:23.149
what curiosity could do

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.470
for the individual.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.263
If you're curious, you'll keep chasing it.

00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:28.573
That's why, you know,
the mantra for K!dult

00:01:28.573 --> 00:01:30.672
is feed your curiosity.

00:01:30.672 --> 00:01:34.523
Individuality and just appreciating, like,

00:01:34.523 --> 00:01:37.705
the way I think and the
way I feel and having,

00:01:37.705 --> 00:01:40.622
you know, letting that
have some value and worth.

00:01:40.622 --> 00:01:43.122
And uh, and depending on that.

00:01:44.296 --> 00:01:46.812
So you know what, if
everybody else is, you know,

00:01:46.812 --> 00:01:48.589
what, what is it, the 405?

00:01:48.589 --> 00:01:49.664
- [Male Host] Mm-hmm.

00:01:49.664 --> 00:01:51.282
- You all got like 29
million highways out here.

00:01:51.282 --> 00:01:52.115
- [Male Host} Yes Sir.
- Right?

00:01:52.115 --> 00:01:53.499
- Take a different route.

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:55.217
If every, if you all got traffic there,

00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:56.709
isn't that what you guys do?

00:01:56.709 --> 00:01:57.798
- [Male Host] Yeah

00:01:57.798 --> 00:02:00.095
- You get off on the next exit,
and you go somewhere else.

00:02:00.095 --> 00:02:02.278
You'd rather sit in the
traffic with the stoplight,

00:02:02.278 --> 00:02:04.333
than to be on the 405 trapped.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:05.267
- [Male Host] Right.
- You know what I'm saying?

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:09.668
- And I think that like,
individuality is a wonderful thing.

00:02:09.668 --> 00:02:11.368
It's the reason why Drake is so good.

00:02:11.368 --> 00:02:13.540
It's the reason why Lorde is so good.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.231
You know, it's the reason why,
you know, the West Coast's

00:02:16.231 --> 00:02:19.648
own Tyler the Creator, now Future,

00:02:19.648 --> 00:02:21.181
and Kendrick Lamar.

00:02:21.181 --> 00:02:23.503
Like, these are all, like,
none of those guys sound alike,

00:02:23.503 --> 00:02:25.763
yet and still, they're
all out at the same time.

00:02:25.763 --> 00:02:26.596
- [Male Host] Right.

00:02:26.596 --> 00:02:29.371
- Doin' their, you know,
perspective things, right?

00:02:29.371 --> 00:02:32.541
So it's betting on yourself
and betting on your difference,

00:02:32.541 --> 00:02:36.401
and appreciating the
rarity of how you think.

00:02:36.401 --> 00:02:38.526
Oh, this is a free world.

00:02:38.526 --> 00:02:41.066
This is a free world now, it's different.

00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:44.360
And there are some restrictions,
but there's no restriction

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.360
in you being exposed to the world to

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:52.089
be discovered as an artist.

00:02:52.089 --> 00:02:54.804
I would say believe in your direction and,

00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:55.637
and do it.

00:02:57.055 --> 00:02:58.570
Spend less time talking about it,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.544
and more time trying to like, perfect it,

00:03:00.544 --> 00:03:05.462
and put it out there, and
you know, if there's fire,

00:03:05.462 --> 00:03:07.589
the kids will sniff it
out, they always do.

00:03:07.589 --> 00:03:09.394
And they'll turn you all the way up

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:11.681
if they feel like that's where you belong.

00:03:11.681 --> 00:03:12.917
That's all, so all you have to do is

00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:14.439
be true to your craft and they see that.

00:03:14.439 --> 00:03:15.832
When they see you going inward,

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:18.204
and being proud of who
you are and what you are,

00:03:18.204 --> 00:03:21.376
and you know, there's some good intention,

00:03:21.376 --> 00:03:22.620
they're going to support you.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.897
It's a numbers game though, right?

00:03:23.897 --> 00:03:25.548
Seven billion people on the planet,

00:03:25.548 --> 00:03:28.465
less than a third online, you know.

00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:31.945
That's a pretty significant number

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:35.778
which raises the odds
for you to become heard.

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:39.701
I mean, even the like, most
boring person on twitter

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:42.678
has a hundred followers, right?

00:03:42.678 --> 00:03:45.964
Hundred people who don't
know you from a can of paint.

00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:50.102
Why, because everything is about numbers.

00:03:50.102 --> 00:03:51.486
So there's benefit, you just have to

00:03:51.486 --> 00:03:53.495
make the numbers work for you.

00:03:53.495 --> 00:03:56.989
We all have egos and it's okay to have

00:03:56.989 --> 00:03:59.736
a sense of self-awareness and identity.

00:03:59.736 --> 00:04:01.134
But it's not okay to have an ego

00:04:01.134 --> 00:04:03.709
because what an ego does is.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:05.536
The ego is a funny, it's a funny,

00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:08.417
it's a funny entity
within the human brain.

00:04:08.417 --> 00:04:11.622
It is on your side when
you are doing great,

00:04:11.622 --> 00:04:14.707
but have you ever noticed
when you think you've failed,

00:04:14.707 --> 00:04:17.834
it's the ego in your mind
that tells you, "it's so bad.

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:19.294
Everyone is looking at you."

00:04:19.294 --> 00:04:22.452
You start to sweat across your
forehead, and you're like,

00:04:22.452 --> 00:04:24.126
"oh my god, I can't
believe this is happening.

00:04:24.126 --> 00:04:26.129
The whole entire world is watching."

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:27.729
And none of it is true.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:31.126
When you're doing great you
shouldn't listen to that voice.

00:04:31.126 --> 00:04:32.349
When you're not doing as great,

00:04:32.349 --> 00:04:35.422
you shouldn't listen to that
voice because it's not good.

00:04:35.422 --> 00:04:38.241
When you're doing great, it's
going to get you hooked on

00:04:38.241 --> 00:04:42.057
that feeling so that when you come down,

00:04:42.057 --> 00:04:44.556
and you, cause we all,
life is a roller coaster.

00:04:44.556 --> 00:04:45.618
There's valleys and peaks.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.884
When you hit that valley,

00:04:46.884 --> 00:04:50.286
if you can't deal then you won't make it.

00:04:50.286 --> 00:04:53.090
We put out the video on November 21st,

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.923
all of a sudden, boom.

00:04:55.948 --> 00:04:56.781
- Boom.

00:04:56.781 --> 00:05:00.204
- I mean, when I say, "boom".
- You mean.

00:05:00.204 --> 00:05:02.376
- [Together] Boom!
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:05:02.376 --> 00:05:04.464
- And we were like, "what's happening?"

00:05:04.464 --> 00:05:05.297
Okay, first of all,

00:05:05.297 --> 00:05:07.196
people are putting out their own videos.

00:05:07.196 --> 00:05:09.336
It was like, no longer my song.

00:05:09.336 --> 00:05:10.440
- I think we actually have your tape.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.617
Can we roll the tape of that?

00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:13.623
When people started putting videos

00:05:13.623 --> 00:05:17.373
around the world of
themselves getting happy.

00:05:19.915 --> 00:05:23.832
("Happy" by Pharrell Williams)

00:05:26.437 --> 00:05:28.854
- [Oprah] Slovakia, Portugal.

00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:46.100
Just amazing.

00:05:57.395 --> 00:05:58.228
Wow...

00:06:10.515 --> 00:06:13.765
Ah, makes me cry too, makes me cry too.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:19.547
Ah, you know, I was just
thinking about your grandmother.

00:06:19.547 --> 00:06:23.245
I bet she didn't even imagine that.

00:06:23.245 --> 00:06:24.078
No...

00:06:26.117 --> 00:06:27.333
No...

00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:30.140
Bet she didn't even imagine that.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:33.648
I know, I know, I know, it's beautiful.

00:06:33.648 --> 00:06:35.398
Beautiful, beautiful!

00:06:36.704 --> 00:06:38.310
- Why am I crying on Oprah?

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.044
(Oprah chuckling)

00:06:40.044 --> 00:06:41.861
- You know, it's being used for

00:06:41.861 --> 00:06:44.819
something that's greater
than yourself, I get that.

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:47.046
- It's overwhelming because it's, like,

00:06:47.046 --> 00:06:49.046
I love what I do and uh,

00:06:50.218 --> 00:06:53.459
I just appreciate the fact that, like,

00:06:53.459 --> 00:06:55.751
people have believed in me for so long,

00:06:55.751 --> 00:06:58.101
that I can make it to this point

00:06:58.101 --> 00:06:58.934
to feel,

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:01.269
to feel that.

00:07:02.278 --> 00:07:03.111
- The Grammy's should also be

00:07:03.111 --> 00:07:05.436
known as a preservation society.

00:07:05.436 --> 00:07:09.694
We don't get recognized for
how expensive our videos are.

00:07:09.694 --> 00:07:12.879
We don't get recognized for, you know,

00:07:12.879 --> 00:07:14.960
the superficial aspects and all the other

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:15.793
ancillary things that come along

00:07:15.793 --> 00:07:18.039
with being in this business.

00:07:18.039 --> 00:07:20.383
You recognize what the first

00:07:20.383 --> 00:07:23.805
and foremost important
thing and that's the music.

00:07:23.805 --> 00:07:26.854
And I'm just so incredibly
humbled and honored

00:07:26.854 --> 00:07:29.604
and appreciative to be a part of,

00:07:30.536 --> 00:07:33.606
you know, this year's
consideration, on any level.

00:07:33.606 --> 00:07:36.035
And to even be doing this interview

00:07:36.035 --> 00:07:38.920
because as I often say, I could be

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.003
just back home in my room

00:07:42.205 --> 00:07:44.680
with uh, with a demo,

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:45.513
or not.

00:07:46.351 --> 00:07:48.043
Could be even worse.

00:07:48.043 --> 00:07:51.706
No music at all and just
a bunch of ideas that I

00:07:51.706 --> 00:07:54.581
could be frustrated to get
out, but with no outlet.

00:07:54.581 --> 00:07:58.270
But instead, it's the complete opposite.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.912
In fact, I'm doing what I love to do.

00:08:00.912 --> 00:08:02.843
You have to be sort of open,

00:08:02.843 --> 00:08:04.760
and I'm not sure, that,

00:08:06.242 --> 00:08:07.651
you know, it's cool to be like,

00:08:07.651 --> 00:08:09.478
super open-minded, you know?

00:08:09.478 --> 00:08:12.826
And then when you are or
when you see people that are,

00:08:12.826 --> 00:08:14.913
everyone like, rolls around like,

00:08:14.913 --> 00:08:17.098
you know, coining them geniuses.

00:08:17.098 --> 00:08:19.163
And really, all it is is just, like,

00:08:19.163 --> 00:08:22.075
being open to other things in the world.

00:08:22.075 --> 00:08:24.826
And I guess that's it, I mean,

00:08:24.826 --> 00:08:26.963
'cause I didn't plan these things.

00:08:26.963 --> 00:08:29.297
I mean now, you know,
in the last few years,

00:08:29.297 --> 00:08:31.422
when I started to catch on,
it started to make sense.

00:08:31.422 --> 00:08:32.856
I'm like, "okay, this is how it goes."

00:08:32.856 --> 00:08:35.158
You sort of, set out these goals.

00:08:35.158 --> 00:08:36.970
And then you have long term goals,

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.029
and then you have short term goals.

00:08:39.029 --> 00:08:42.144
And somewhere within, you know,

00:08:42.144 --> 00:08:43.498
you find your hobbies and then

00:08:43.498 --> 00:08:47.505
you find the things that
you are interested in,

00:08:47.505 --> 00:08:48.986
which are two separate things

00:08:48.986 --> 00:08:50.426
because what your hobbies are,

00:08:50.426 --> 00:08:52.366
what you naturally and readily go to

00:08:52.366 --> 00:08:53.599
and what you're interested in

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:56.261
are the things that you don't
mind studying a little more

00:08:56.261 --> 00:08:58.882
and researching a little more
than you would anything else.

00:08:58.882 --> 00:09:02.313
And somewhere at the 50 yard line,

00:09:02.313 --> 00:09:04.320
is what you should be
doing with your life.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.972
I was lucky enough to stumble across that.

00:09:07.972 --> 00:09:11.583
I look at every opportunity
as something that

00:09:11.583 --> 00:09:14.985
cements the next block in your sidewalk.

00:09:14.985 --> 00:09:16.869
And it's just important to like, you know,

00:09:16.869 --> 00:09:18.880
don't look over your
shoulder every five minutes.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.605
If anything, just continue to

00:09:20.605 --> 00:09:24.432
stand on your tippy toes
and see how far you can see

00:09:24.432 --> 00:09:27.214
and what's on the horizon.

00:09:27.214 --> 00:09:29.138
When you say that you come from nothing,

00:09:29.138 --> 00:09:31.116
you're saying,

00:09:31.116 --> 00:09:33.538
you're saying, "I come from nothing".

00:09:33.538 --> 00:09:35.667
Like, you know, nothing.

00:09:35.667 --> 00:09:37.846
You know what nothing is?

00:09:37.846 --> 00:09:39.353
Nothing is a void.

00:09:39.353 --> 00:09:40.270
Nothing is,

00:09:41.278 --> 00:09:43.361
deep space with no stars.

00:09:44.886 --> 00:09:45.803
Nothing is,

00:09:47.652 --> 00:09:50.479
it's not possible for
you to exist in nothing.

00:09:50.479 --> 00:09:52.816
So there's no such thing as nothing.

00:09:52.816 --> 00:09:54.609
It's all relative.

00:09:54.609 --> 00:09:56.656
So when you say, you know
I was explaining this

00:09:56.656 --> 00:09:59.875
to an old friend the other
day who was saying, you know,

00:09:59.875 --> 00:10:02.175
"I have it so hard, it's so tough.

00:10:02.175 --> 00:10:04.464
It's so crazy," and, you know,

00:10:04.464 --> 00:10:05.955
"I don't have anything."

00:10:05.955 --> 00:10:07.345
Well, sure you do.

00:10:07.345 --> 00:10:09.952
You have the same things
that Bill Gates has,

00:10:09.952 --> 00:10:13.035
because, just like him, when he dies,

00:10:13.872 --> 00:10:16.519
you know what you're
going to take with you?

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:18.112
That's exactly right.

00:10:18.112 --> 00:10:21.362
So while the apartment may smell funny,

00:10:22.648 --> 00:10:24.941
while there may be pests and rodents,

00:10:24.941 --> 00:10:26.960
'cause that's what people generally

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.639
relate to nothing, right?

00:10:28.639 --> 00:10:29.992
Come from nothing.

00:10:29.992 --> 00:10:33.881
And the car is a Hyundai,
if there is a car.

00:10:33.881 --> 00:10:36.241
Or there's bus tickets or,
- Train.

00:10:36.241 --> 00:10:38.131
- Train and free lunch.

00:10:38.131 --> 00:10:39.773
Nothing, right?

00:10:39.773 --> 00:10:41.745
It's not true.

00:10:41.745 --> 00:10:45.401
Imagine the people who
live out in remote villages

00:10:45.401 --> 00:10:48.234
that have never heard of the iPod.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:53.147
But they're happy and
they would argue with you

00:10:53.147 --> 00:10:55.616
when you tell them that they have nothing.

00:10:55.616 --> 00:10:58.381
Nothing is a perception.

00:10:58.381 --> 00:11:03.189
So if your reality is
merely just your perception

00:11:03.189 --> 00:11:08.015
and a perspective, these
things are interchangeable.

00:11:08.015 --> 00:11:09.848
So first, you need to,

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:12.784
when you say you come from nothing,

00:11:12.784 --> 00:11:15.202
you should probably reevaluate
that and think about it

00:11:15.202 --> 00:11:17.306
because if you're not cancer-stricken,

00:11:17.306 --> 00:11:19.024
and you don't have AIDS,

00:11:19.024 --> 00:11:22.183
and you have your legs and your fingers,

00:11:22.183 --> 00:11:24.627
I'd say you have a
bigger chance than some,

00:11:24.627 --> 00:11:28.086
you have a huge advantage over people

00:11:28.086 --> 00:11:32.956
who are amputees, who are
really doing good in life.

00:11:32.956 --> 00:11:35.478
And people who have
had AIDS for ten years,

00:11:35.478 --> 00:11:37.085
who are really succeeding.

00:11:37.085 --> 00:11:39.745
And people who have cancer
that are fighting everyday.

00:11:39.745 --> 00:11:42.408
But still they are working at

00:11:42.408 --> 00:11:44.962
the things that they love to do in life.

00:11:44.962 --> 00:11:47.254
I would say you have
a little bit more than

00:11:47.254 --> 00:11:49.429
what you thought was nothing.

00:11:49.429 --> 00:11:52.268
How many times have you guys, you know,

00:11:52.268 --> 00:11:56.019
in a situation of need, a moment of need,

00:11:56.019 --> 00:11:56.991
you go, "you know what I need?

00:11:56.991 --> 00:11:58.845
I need da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da."

00:11:58.845 --> 00:12:01.798
Right, and you blow it off.

00:12:01.798 --> 00:12:05.883
And then three years later,
there's a guy, you know,

00:12:05.883 --> 00:12:09.856
on an infomercial describing
exactly your idea.

00:12:09.856 --> 00:12:13.297
And he's showing you how
great your product works.

00:12:13.297 --> 00:12:17.292
You have to be unafraid to
listen and unafraid to think that

00:12:17.292 --> 00:12:19.675
something that you have is a good idea.

00:12:19.675 --> 00:12:23.092
Now, you also can't believe in it so much

00:12:24.776 --> 00:12:28.943
that you have deaf ears toward
like, reason and reality.

00:12:30.531 --> 00:12:33.943
Because you can have the most
brilliant idea in the world,

00:12:33.943 --> 00:12:36.038
but you got to bet on yourself.

